<h2>What a camera inspection is</h2>
<p>A waterproof camera on a flexible push cable goes into the sewer through a cleanout, usually in the basement, crawlspace or yard. As it travels toward the main, a counter on the screen tracks distance, and a small transmitter in the camera head lets us find its position and depth from the surface with a handheld locator. The result is a video of the whole line and a set of marks on the ground showing where each problem sits.</p>
<p>We invite owners to watch the monitor while we work, and we explain what is on screen as it comes up. You get the recording afterward.</p>

<h2>What we look for</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Pipe material and transitions.</strong> Many Front Range laterals change material along the way: cast iron under the house, clay tile or Orangeburg in the yard, and sometimes a newer PVC patch where someone made a past repair.</li>
  <li><strong>Roots.</strong> Fine hair roots at a joint, or a dense mat that fills the pipe. Silver maple, cottonwood and Siberian elm are frequent culprits in older neighborhoods.</li>
  <li><strong>Offsets and separated joints.</strong> Expansive clay lifts and drops pipe as it wets and dries. Joints end up misaligned or pulled apart.</li>
  <li><strong>Bellies and lost grade.</strong> Standing water in a stretch of pipe tells us the soil has settled and the line is no longer falling steadily.</li>
  <li><strong>Corrosion and cracking.</strong> Cast iron rusts from the inside, often wearing through along the bottom. Clay cracks along its length or around a joint.</li>
  <li><strong>Deformation.</strong> Orangeburg that is losing its round shape under soil load.</li>
  <li><strong>The connection at the main.</strong> Whether the tap is intact, offset, or damaged, since in most Front Range cities and districts that part is the owner's responsibility.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Why the camera decides the method</h2>
<p>The same backup can come from very different causes, and each points to a different fix. Roots in a sound pipe may only need <a href="/hydro-jetting/">hydro jetting</a>. Cracked pipe that still holds its shape and slope is a candidate for <a href="/cipp-pipe-lining/">CIPP lining</a>. Crushed or deformed pipe calls for <a href="/pipe-bursting/">pipe bursting</a>. A belly or a stretch that has lost grade usually means a <a href="/sewer-line-replacement/">spot repair or replacement</a>.</p>
<p>A quote written without footage is a guess, and on a sewer a wrong guess costs twice. We would rather spend the time to see the whole line and give you a recommendation we can defend.</p>
<p>Sometimes the first pass is incomplete because roots or debris block the view. In that case we clean the line and run the camera again. Cleaning often uncovers cracks or offsets that were hidden, and it is better to learn about them before a repair is scoped than halfway through one.</p>

<h2>When to schedule an inspection</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Repeat backups or slow drains.</strong> Especially a basement floor drain that gurgles or backs up when the washer drains.</li>
  <li><strong>Before buying a house.</strong> Sewer condition is rarely covered by a standard home inspection, and older Front Range homes can carry expensive surprises in the yard. It is worth knowing before closing.</li>
  <li><strong>After a neighbor's line fails.</strong> Postwar subdivisions were built quickly with the same materials and the same trees. Failures tend to cluster.</li>
  <li><strong>Before a remodel.</strong> If you are finishing a basement or pouring a new driveway, check the sewer before it is buried under new work.</li>
  <li><strong>After a repair.</strong> A post-work video confirms the job was done right, and gives you a record for the next buyer.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Colorado conditions we keep in mind</h2>
<h3>Depth and access</h3>
<p>Because sewers here run below frost depth, a lateral can sit many feet down at the property line and deeper still at the main. Locating tells us exactly how deep, which affects whether a pit is practical and where it should go. We also check whether an inside cleanout exists, since it can make lining possible without any digging.</p>

<h3>Who owns what</h3>
<p>In most Front Range cities and districts, the owner is responsible for the lateral from the house to the main, including the section under the street. When the camera shows a defect past the curb, it is usually still yours to fix. We mark where it is so you can plan for it.</p>

<h3>District requirements</h3>
<p>The Denver metro has a patchwork of city utilities and special sanitation districts, and some of them want to see video before and after certain kinds of work. We find out what your city or district requires before a repair is booked.</p>

<h2>What you receive</h2>
<p>Every inspection ends with the video, the location and depth of what we found, and a plain explanation of your options, including when the answer is to do nothing yet. If a repair makes sense, you get a written scope with the method and the reasoning. See how an inspection fits into the larger job on <a href="/our-process/">our process page</a>, or browse the <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a>. We serve the whole metro and Front Range; the list is on our <a href="/service-areas/">service areas page</a>.</p>
